'''''Camelina''''' is a [[genus]] within the [[flowering plant]] family [[Brassicaceae]]. The ''Camelina'' species, commonly known as false flax, are native to [[Mediterranean]] regions of [[Europe]] and [[Asia]]. Most species of this genus have been little studied, with the exception of ''[[Camelina sativa]]'', historically cultivated as an [[oil plant]]. [[Heinrich Johann Nepomuk von Crantz]] was the first botanist to use the genus ''Camelina'' in his classification works in 1762. 

As a way to reduce fossil fuel emissions, the US Navy tested a 50-50 mix of jet aviation fuel and biofuel derived from camelina seeds in 2010.<ref>{{Cite journal |title=From Seed to Supersonic |url=http://greenfleet.dodlive.mil/files/2011/01/Win11_From_Seed_To_Supersonic.pdf |journal=Currents |publisher=US Navy |volume=Winter 2011 |access-date=2016-11-05 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180219022719/http://greenfleet.dodlive.mil/files/2011/01/Win11_From_Seed_To_Supersonic.pdf |archive-date=2018-02-19 |url-status=dead}}</ref> A study published in December 2016 explained that the current low price of conventional [[kerosene]]-based jet fuel makes it cost-prohibitive for commercial airlines to use camelina-based jet fuel. The study said substantial government intervention would be one way to create a market for camelina, by combining 9 percent government subsidy on camelina crop production, with 9 percent tax on  conventional fuel.<ref>{{Cite journal |title=Camelina oil could be economically feasible as source of commercial jet fuel, new OSU analysis shows |url=http://extension.oregonstate.edu/news/release/2017/02/camelina-oil-could-be-economically-feasible-source-commercial-jet-fuel-new-osu-analy |publisher=Oregon State University Extension and Agricultural Research News |date=2017-02-17}}</ref>

== Etymology ==
The name ''Camelina'' comes from the Greek for "ground" and "flax", alluding to its being a weed which suppresses the vigour of flax crops.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://florabase.dec.wa.gov.au/browse/profile/21468 |title=FloraBase—the Western Australian Flora |first=Western Australian Herbarium, Biodiversity and Conservation Science, Department of Biodiversity, Conservation and |last=Attractions |website=florabase.dec.wa.gov.au |access-date=2012-12-12 |archive-date=2011-03-28 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110328201031/http://florabase.dec.wa.gov.au/browse/profile/21468 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

== Description ==
Camelina plants are [[annual plant|annual]] or [[biennial plant|biennial]] [[herbs]]. Their leaves are [[Leaf shape|simple]], [[Leaf shape|lanceolate]] to narrowly [[Leaf shape|elliptic]]. The flowers are [[Hermaphrodite (botany)|hermaphroditic]] [[actinomorphic]], grouped in [[racemes]], and yellowish colored.  The seeds are formed in [[Dehiscence (botany)|dehiscent siliques]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.plantes-botanique.org/genre_Camelina |title=Plantes et botanique – le genre Camelina |website=Plantes et botanique|date=27 May 2020 }}</ref>

