== Biodiesel ==
Biodiesel made from camelina has a variety of benefits. First, traditional petroleum or diesel fuel is not renewable resources, the production of these resources is finite. Camelina biodiesel, however, is a renewable resource. Camelina-based aviation fuel could save 84% of carbon emissions.<ref>{{cite web |title=Camelina jet fuel could cut carbon emissions by 84 percent |publisher=phys.org |access-date=2020-05-19 |url=https://phys.org/news/2009-07-camelina-jet-fuel-carbon-emissions.html}}</ref> Camelina biodiesel can be produced in large quantities as feedstocks are enough. Moreover, camelina biodiesel can reduce a country's dependence on fossil resources, which can ensure a country's energy security. In addition, camelina biodiesel is an environmentally friendly fuel, and it is biodegradable.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Quan |first1=He |title=An evaluation of biodiesel production from Camelina sativa grown in Nova Scotia. |journal=Industrial Crops & Products |date=2016 |volume=81 |pages=162–168 |doi=10.1016/j.indcrop.2015.11.073}}</ref> The [[greenhouse gas emissions|greenhouse gas emission]] of camelina biodiesel produced by [[no-till farming]] is lower than that of traditional methods.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Dangol |first1=Namrata |title=Life-cycle energy, GHG and cost comparison of camelina-based biodiesel and bio jet fuel |journal=Boules |pages=1–9 |doi=10.1080/17597269.2017.1369632 |year=2017 |volume=11 |issue=4 |s2cid=135275011}}</ref>

